    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_5f070ed6ab50e2ca1be81984058f0421bc43270c9775e402cb71926757e0cf4f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Early zero computation separates the frequency and direction of Gram-law failures and identifies close-root intervals with small intervening extrema as poorly conditioned sign-detection cases. Later Lehmer criteria turn close-pair geometry into a deformation witness only after normalizing it against the inverse-square field of all other zeros; the squared gap sets the leading bound scale while the environment remains an admissibility obligation.",
